\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Selection and invertibility are separate factors.} Top row: a client's private batch of eight drone frames, two of which (red) show the rooftop solar panels the attacker asked for. Below: HF-GradInv reconstructions from one shared gradient when the client model is built on vanilla CLIP or on the adversarially fine-tuned TeCoA-4 encoder, each under an honest classifier head and under the head Geminio\xspace  rigs for the query. The honest head on TeCoA-4 reconstructs the whole batch moderately; the rigged head on TeCoA-4 reconstructs the two targets sharply and sacrifices the rest; on vanilla CLIP neither head recovers anything. Same seed throughout.}}{2}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:teaser}{{1}{2}{\textbf {Selection and invertibility are separate factors.} Top row: a client's private batch of eight drone frames, two of which (red) show the rooftop solar panels the attacker asked for. Below: HF-GradInv reconstructions from one shared gradient when the client model is built on vanilla CLIP or on the adversarially fine-tuned TeCoA-4 encoder, each under an honest classifier head and under the head \geminio rigs for the query. The honest head on TeCoA-4 reconstructs the whole batch moderately; the rigged head on TeCoA-4 reconstructs the two targets sharply and sacrifices the rest; on vanilla CLIP neither head recovers anything. Same seed throughout}{figure.caption.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:teaser@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][1][]2}{}{}{}}
